  "goldenVersion": "nls-golden-stage1-v1",
  "derivedFrom": "docs/evidence/.local/nls-benchmark-dryrun.json (nls-golden-v1) — the same 35 queries, in the same order.",
  "why": "The dry-run file holds request bodies for a Stage 2 prompt that names the user's cities, which nls-plan.md §5.5 keeps out of Stage 1 until security-privacy has ruled on sending localities to the provider. The queries carry over; the prompt and the expected shape do not. The dry run also carried no expected answers at all — these are hand-labelled here, before any prompt was tuned.",
  "shape": {
    "category": "accepted values; null means 'the query does not state one'",
    "tags": "accepted tag SETS, order-insensitive",
    "visit": "accepted values",
    "origin": "accepted values",
    "keyword": "accepted strings, compared after normalise(); null means 'no keyword'; \"ANY\" accepts any grounded keyword"
  },
  "scoring": {
    "no-false-filter": "Every enum-axis value the model ASSERTED (a non-null category, each tag, a non-'all' visit or origin) must appear in the accepted values for its field. Asserting fewer is not a failure: under-filtering is the safe direction, over-filtering silently hides the row the user wanted. Gate: >= 90%.",
    "exact": "Every field matches an accepted value, keyword included. Gate: >= 70%, Hebrew and English reported separately.",
    "measured-after-the-clamp": "Both are scored on the intent the ROUTE returns, i.e. after the clamp. That is the number that describes what a user could see. The raw pre-clamp reply is recorded per case as well, so 'the model invented it and the clamp caught it' stays distinguishable from 'the model did not invent it'."
  },
